#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# chinese_remainder

#% text
# `chinese_remainder(rs, ms)` gives the least non-negative `x` with `x = r_i mod m_i` for every `i`; `chinese_remainder(rs, ms, d)` the least such `x >= d`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The moduli need not be coprime; when the congruences are inconsistent the call stays unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
chinese_remainder([3, 4], [4, 5])
#% output
#= 19

#% cell
chinese_remainder([2, 3, 5], [3, 5, 7])
#% output
#= 68

#% cell
chinese_remainder([1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], 100)
#% output
#= 117

#% cell
chinese_remainder([1, 2], [4, 6])
#% output
#= chinese_remainder([1, 2], [4, 6])

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `modular_inverse`, `extended_gcd`, `power_mod`
